      "source": [
        "---\n",
        "title: \"Seeing the Unseeable: Visualizing, Clustering, and Classifying High-Dimensional Data\"\n",
        "description: \"A beginner-friendly walkthrough of PCA, MDS, t-SNE, K-means, and logistic regression — from simulated data to a real chemistry dataset\"\n",
        "date: 2026-07-24\n",
        "categories: [Python, machine-learning, dimensionality-reduction]\n",
        "jupyter: python3\n",
        "execute:\n",
        "  warning: false\n",
        "  message: false\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Acknowledgment\n",
        "The code in this post is adapted from a recitation notebook for the **Machine Learning with Python: From Linear Models to Deep Learning** course, part of the MITx MicroMasters Program in Statistics and Data Science. I've rewritten it here as an annotated, from-scratch teaching walkthrough, with plain-language explanations of both the code and the results.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## The questions this post answers\n",
        "\n",
        "Imagine you've measured **100 different things** about each of several thousand samples — maybe gene activity levels, or chemical concentrations, or pixel brightness values. Each sample is a point floating in 100-dimensional space. Humans can't picture 100 dimensions. We can barely picture 3. So before we can even ask \"are there natural groups in this data?\", we need a way to compress that 100-dimensional cloud down into something we can actually look at — usually 2 dimensions, a normal scatter plot.\n",
        "\n",
        "This post works through that whole problem, end to end, using a mix of **simulated data** (where we secretly know the right answer, so we can check our work) and a **real dataset** (wine chemistry). Specifically, we'll investigate:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. **Can you trust a plain scatter plot of high-dimensional data?** (Spoiler: usually not — we'll show why.)\n",
        "2. **Do different dimensionality-reduction methods (PCA, MDS, t-SNE) agree with each other, and does it matter which one you pick?**\n",
        "3. **Can an algorithm that doesn't know the \"true\" groups (K-means) figure out how many groups there really are, using only diagnostic statistics?**\n",
        "4. **Applied to real wine chemistry data: can unsupervised clustering rediscover the 3 known grape varieties, using chemistry alone, with no labels?**\n",
        "5. **Can a classifier trained on just 2 of the 13 chemical measurements do almost as well as one trained on all 13 — and can we find out mathematically which 2 measurements matter most?**\n",
        "\n",
        "Every code block below has two parts: a **plain-language explanation before the code** (what we're about to do and why), and an **interpretation after the plot or output** (what the result actually tells us, explained the way you'd explain it to a curious high schooler, not a statistics professor).\n",
        "\n",
        "## Setup"
      ],
      "id": "f6ca827f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
      ],
      "id": "545c2d89",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Two tools, two jobs. `numpy` (imported as `np`) is for working with grids of numbers — think of it as a supercharged version of a spreadsheet that Python can do math on quickly. `matplotlib.pyplot` (imported as `plt`) is what actually draws the charts you'll see throughout this post.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Part 1 — Building \"fake\" data where we already know the answer\n",
        "\n",
        "Before trying these techniques on real, messy data, it's smart to first try them on data we built ourselves — because then we already know the correct answer, and we can check whether the method actually found it.\n",
        "\n",
        "Here's the plan: we'll invent **4 make-believe groups** of data points, each living in **100 dimensions** (think of 100 as \"100 different measurements per sample\" — could be 100 genes, 100 chemical readings, anything). Each group will be centered in a different part of that 100-dimensional space, so a perfect method should be able to tell all 4 groups apart."
      ],
      "id": "3cdf1fb3"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from scipy.stats import ortho_group  # generates random rotation matrices"
      ],
      "id": "094cd076",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`ortho_group` is a tool for generating a random **rotation** — a mathematical operation that spins a shape around without stretching or squishing it, kind of like rotating a photograph without warping it. We'll use this in a minute to make one of our 4 groups look more \"realistic\" (tilted at a random angle) instead of perfectly lined up with the axes, the way real-world data usually is."
      ],
      "id": "193cb413"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Draws random points from `n // (# groups)` samples per group, from a multivariate\n",
        "# normal (\"bell curve\" in many dimensions) distribution with given means and shapes.\n",
        "def sample_gmm(n, mu, cov, return_labels=True):\n",
        "    data = np.vstack([np.random.multivariate_normal(mu[i], cov[i], size=n//mu.shape[0]) for i in range(mu.shape[0])])\n",
        "    labels = np.hstack([i*np.ones(n//mu.shape[0]) for i in range(mu.shape[0])])\n",
        "    perm = np.random.permutation(data.shape[0])\n",
        "    if return_labels:\n",
        "        return data[perm], labels[perm]\n",
        "    return data[perm]"
      ],
      "id": "e33e28bf",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Think of this function as a **random point generator with a memory of which group each point belongs to**. In plain terms:\n",
        "\n",
        "- For each of our 4 groups, it randomly scatters points around that group's center, the same way a dartboard scatters darts around a bullseye — some land dead center, most land a bit off, a few land far off.\n",
        "- It keeps a label for every point recording which group it \"belongs to\" — this is our **ground truth**, the answer key we'll use later to check whether our methods got it right.\n",
        "- It shuffles all the points together at the end, so the data doesn't come \"pre-sorted\" by group (which would be cheating — real data never comes conveniently sorted for you)."
      ],
      "id": "7a02e5e1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "d = 100\n",
        "mu = np.zeros((4,d))\n",
        "mu[0,1:]=1\n",
        "mu[1,1:40]=1\n",
        "mu[1,40:]=0.5\n",
        "mu[2,1:]=-1\n",
        "mu[3,1:95]=-1\n",
        "mu[3,96]=2"
      ],
      "id": "6de41c48",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Here we're setting the **center point** (\"bullseye\") of each of our 4 groups, each one somewhere different in our 100-dimensional space. Group 0 sits at one location, group 1 at another, and so on — each group gets a different pattern of high/low values across the 100 measurements, so no two groups occupy the same territory."
      ],
      "id": "d62155a9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "np.random.seed(350)\n",
        "d_cov=np.zeros((4,d,d))\n",
        "d_cov[0] = 0.05*np.eye(d)\n",
        "rot = ortho_group.rvs(d)\n",
        "mat = np.eye(d)\n",
        "mat[0,0] = 3.0\n",
        "mat[1,1] = 1.0\n",
        "d_cov[1] = np.dot(rot, np.dot(mat,rot.T))\n",
        "d_cov[2] = 0.5*np.eye(d)\n",
        "d_cov[3] = 0.02*np.eye(d)\n",
        "cov = d_cov"
      ],
      "id": "7dae1b98",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`np.random.seed(350)` is like writing down the exact starting position of a dice roll — it makes our \"random\" data **reproducible**, so anyone who runs this code gets the identical result. Always do this when you want to be able to debug or reproduce your own work later.\n",
        "\n",
        "The rest of this block controls how **spread out and what shape** each group is:\n",
        "\n",
        "- Groups 0 and 3 are small, tight, round little clusters (imagine a tight huddle of people).\n",
        "- Group 2 is a bigger, but still round, cluster (a looser huddle).\n",
        "- Group 1 is stretched into a cigar/football shape and then tilted at a random angle (using the rotation tool from before) — this is meant to look more like messy real-world data, where clusters aren't neat circles."
      ],
      "id": "f7a369d4"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X,y = sample_gmm(1200,mu,cov)\n",
        "print(\"Data shape:\", X.shape)\n",
        "print(\"Number of groups:\", len(np.unique(y)))"
      ],
      "id": "5b9a8995",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Now we actually generate the data: 1,200 total points (300 per group), scattered across 100 dimensions. `X` is our giant data table (1,200 rows, 100 columns); `y` is the secret answer key telling us which of the 4 groups each row really belongs to.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Part 2 — Why a normal scatter plot fails here"
      ],
      "id": "35ad42b0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12,5))\n",
        "axes[0].scatter(X[:,0],X[:,1])\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"Raw scatter (no group info)\",size=14)\n",
        "axes[0].set_xlabel(\"x1\",size=12)\n",
        "axes[0].set_ylabel(\"x2\",size=12)\n",
        "axes[0].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[1].scatter(X[:,0],X[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"Raw scatter (colored by TRUE group)\",size=14)\n",
        "axes[1].set_xlabel(\"x1\",size=12)\n",
        "axes[1].set_ylabel(\"x2\",size=12)\n",
        "axes[1].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "26c86e66",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This plots just the **first 2 out of our 100 measurements** — literally just picking 2 columns out of the data table and plotting them against each other, since that's all a normal scatter plot can show.\n",
        "\n",
        "**How to read this plot:** on the left, every dot is the same color because we're pretending we don't know the groups. On the right, we've secretly colored each dot by its true group, just to see what we're missing. If you're looking at this and thinking \"it just looks like a blob, I can't tell the groups apart even *with* the colors turned on\" — that's exactly the point being made. **Two dimensions out of 100 essentially never happen to be the two dimensions where the real separation between groups is visible.** It's like trying to tell four different countries apart by looking only at their average rainfall and average temperature — you're throwing away 98 other pieces of information that might actually distinguish them.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Part 3 — PCA: finding the \"best\" 2D summary\n",
        "\n",
        "**Plain-language idea:** instead of picking 2 of our original 100 measurements at random, what if we let the computer search for the *best possible* 2 new \"measurements\" — ones specifically chosen to spread the data out as much as possible? That's what PCA (Principal Component Analysis) does."
      ],
      "id": "40a43ceb"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA\n",
        "\n",
        "pca = PCA()\n",
        "z = pca.fit_transform(X)"
      ],
      "id": "2b6d564d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`PCA()` creates the tool; `.fit_transform(X)` does two things at once — it studies our 100-dimensional data to find the best new \"viewing angles\" (called **principal components**), and then immediately re-expresses every data point in terms of those new angles. The result, `z`, is a new table the same size as `X`, but now the *first* column captures the most spread-out view of the data, the second column the next-most, and so on."
      ],
      "id": "be24623d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.scatter(z[:,0],z[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "plt.title(\"First 2 Principal Components (colored by true group)\",size=16)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"PC 1\",size=13)\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"PC 2\",size=13)\n",
        "plt.axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "1249c85b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this plot:** unlike the raw scatter plot from before, you should now clearly see the 4 groups separated — likely as 2 large, well-separated blobs and 1 or 2 smaller ones. This is a big deal: PCA managed to find a 2D \"camera angle\" on our 100-dimensional data that actually reveals the true structure, without ever being told what the true groups were. It did this purely by hunting for the directions where the data varies the most.\n",
        "\n",
        "### How many \"principal components\" should we actually keep?\n",
        "\n",
        "If PC1 and PC2 already show clear groups, do we need PC3, PC4, ... PC100 at all? Here are three ways to decide, explained simply:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Method 1 — The \"elbow\" plot.** Plot how much information (variance) each component captures, in order. Early components usually capture a lot; later ones capture almost nothing. We're looking for the point where the curve flattens out — like the elbow of a bent arm."
      ],
      "id": "a3917827"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8,5))\n",
        "plt.plot(np.arange(1,101),pca.explained_variance_ratio_[0:100],marker='o',markersize=3)\n",
        "plt.title(\"% of Information (Variance) Captured by Each Component\",size=15)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Component #\",size=12)\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Fraction of Variance Explained\",size=12)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "54fcac0c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this plot:** the first component captures far more information than any single one of our original 100 measurements would on its own — that's the whole point of PCA. Notice how quickly the curve drops and then flattens near zero — that flat tail means components 5, 6, 7... onward are basically capturing noise, not real structure. This tells us we could safely summarize our whole 100-dimensional dataset using just a handful of components without losing much real information.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Method 2 — Cumulative variance threshold.** Instead of eyeballing an elbow, just decide \"I want to keep 85% of the total information\" and let the math tell us exactly how many components that requires."
      ],
      "id": "67c8f325"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "cumulative = np.cumsum(pca.explained_variance_ratio_)\n",
        "n_needed = np.where(cumulative >= .85)[0][0] + 1\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8,5))\n",
        "plt.plot(np.arange(1,101),cumulative,marker='o',markersize=3)\n",
        "plt.axhline(0.85, color='red', linestyle='--', label='85% threshold')\n",
        "plt.axvline(n_needed, color='red', linestyle='--')\n",
        "plt.title(\"Cumulative Variance Explained\",size=15)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Number of Components\",size=12)\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Cumulative Fraction of Variance Explained\",size=12)\n",
        "plt.legend()\n",
        "plt.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Components needed to explain 85% of the variance: {n_needed}\")"
      ],
      "id": "1f8bfdd6",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this:** the red dashed lines mark where our running total first crosses 85%. Whatever number gets printed above tells us: \"you can throw away all but this many components and still keep 85% of the real information in the original 100-dimensional dataset.\" That's a genuinely useful data-compression result — and it means most of our 100 original measurements were, in a sense, redundant with each other.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Part 4 — MDS: a different philosophy for the same goal\n",
        "\n",
        "**Plain-language idea:** PCA looks for directions of maximum spread. MDS (Multidimensional Scaling) takes a totally different approach: it tries to place points on a 2D page so that **the distances between them on the page match, as closely as possible, how far apart they really are** in the original 100-dimensional space. It's like trying to flatten a globe into a 2D map while distorting distances as little as possible."
      ],
      "id": "df06777a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.manifold import MDS\n",
        "\n",
        "mds = MDS(n_components=2, eps=1e-5, n_init=4)\n",
        "mds_embedding = mds.fit(X)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(mds_embedding.embedding_[:,0],mds_embedding.embedding_[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "plt.title(\"MDS Plot (colored by true group)\",size=16)\n",
        "plt.axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "7b8d7b96",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this plot:** you should again see the same 4 groups emerge as distinct clusters, though the plot may look \"stretched\" or \"flipped\" differently from the PCA plot — that's completely normal and not a mistake. What matters is *which points are grouped with which other points*, not the exact orientation of the plot; MDS has no reason to line up with PCA's particular choice of axes.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Technical note\n",
        "scikit-learn's `MDS` uses a \"stress\" minimization approach, which makes it a genuinely different, nonlinear method — mathematically, it is **not** the same as PCA, even though a related method called \"classical MDS\" (common in R) actually *is* mathematically equivalent to PCA. If you're comparing code between R and Python, don't assume `MDS()` gives identical results in both languages.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Part 5 — t-SNE: built for finding tight local clusters\n",
        "\n",
        "**Plain-language idea:** t-SNE takes yet another approach — instead of trying to preserve *all* pairwise distances (like MDS) or maximize spread (like PCA), it focuses specifically on preserving **who's in whose immediate neighborhood**. It's very good at pulling tight clusters apart visually, but — and this is important — **the distance *between* clusters on a t-SNE plot doesn't mean anything reliable.** Two clusters that look close together in a t-SNE plot might actually be very far apart in the real data, or vice versa."
      ],
      "id": "7b706dd1"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.manifold import TSNE\n",
        "\n",
        "tsne_40 = TSNE(n_components=2, perplexity=40, random_state=0)\n",
        "z_tsne_40 = tsne_40.fit_transform(X)\n",
        "\n",
        "tsne_5 = TSNE(n_components=2, perplexity=5, random_state=0)\n",
        "z_tsne_5 = tsne_5.fit_transform(X)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13,5.5))\n",
        "axes[0].scatter(z_tsne_40[:,0],z_tsne_40[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"t-SNE, perplexity 40\",size=15)\n",
        "axes[0].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[1].scatter(z_tsne_5[:,0],z_tsne_5[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"t-SNE, perplexity 5\",size=15)\n",
        "axes[1].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "5c672aaa",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`perplexity` is t-SNE's main dial to turn — loosely, it controls how many \"neighbors\" each point pays attention to when deciding what counts as its local neighborhood.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Interpreting this plot:** compare the two panels. With **perplexity 40** (more neighbors considered), the 4 groups usually appear as smoother, more clearly separated blobs. With **perplexity 5** (very few neighbors considered), the algorithm zooms in so tightly on local structure that a single true group can get visually fragmented into multiple smaller-looking pieces, or the overall picture can look noisier. Neither setting is \"the correct one\" in an absolute sense — this is why it's good practice to try a few different perplexity values and sanity-check the result, rather than trusting a single run blindly.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Combining PCA and t-SNE\n",
        "\n",
        "A common, genuinely useful trick: use PCA first to strip out low-information \"noise\" dimensions, then hand the *cleaned-up* data to t-SNE."
      ],
      "id": "0eddf8c2"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "tsne_on_pca = TSNE(n_components=2, perplexity=40, random_state=0)\n",
        "z_tsne_pca = tsne_on_pca.fit_transform(z[:,0:10])\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.scatter(z_tsne_pca[:,0],z_tsne_pca[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "plt.title(\"t-SNE run on the first 10 Principal Components\",size=16)\n",
        "plt.axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fb4854c1",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this:** notice how much crisper and tighter these 4 clusters look compared to running t-SNE on the full, raw 100-dimensional data. That's because the first 10 principal components already contain almost all the real signal (we saw that in the elbow plot earlier) — feeding t-SNE this \"denoised\" version lets it focus entirely on real structure instead of getting distracted by noisy, uninformative dimensions.\n",
        "\n",
        "### When does t-SNE clearly beat PCA?\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's build a harder test case: **25 separate groups** instead of 4, each one tucked into a different single dimension."
      ],
      "id": "fb91c699"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "mu2 = np.zeros((25,100))\n",
        "for i in range(25):\n",
        "    mu2[i,i] = 1\n",
        "cov2 = [0.02*np.eye(100) for _ in range(25)]\n",
        "X2,y2 = sample_gmm(1000,mu2,cov2)\n",
        "\n",
        "pca2 = PCA()\n",
        "z2 = pca2.fit_transform(X2)\n",
        "\n",
        "tsne2 = TSNE(n_components=2, perplexity=30, random_state=0)\n",
        "z2_tsne = tsne2.fit_transform(X2)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13,5.5))\n",
        "axes[0].scatter(z2[:,0],z2[:,1],c=y2,cmap='tab20')\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"PCA on 25-group data\",size=15)\n",
        "axes[0].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[1].scatter(z2_tsne[:,0],z2_tsne[:,1],c=y2,cmap='tab20')\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"t-SNE on 25-group data\",size=15)\n",
        "axes[1].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "e4d23783",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this:** on the left (PCA), the 25 groups are mostly smeared together into a blob, since no single pair of components can capture 25 separate directions of separation at once. On the right (t-SNE), the 25 groups typically pull apart into distinct little clusters. **The lesson:** PCA is at its best when the important structure in your data is genuinely \"big-picture\" and spread across just a few dominant directions. t-SNE is at its best when you have many small, tightly-packed groups that don't line up neatly with just a few directions. Neither tool is \"better\" in general — it depends on the shape of your data.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Part 6 — K-means: letting the computer find groups on its own\n",
        "\n",
        "Everything so far has been about *visualizing* groups we already knew about. Now let's flip it around: **pretend we don't know the answer**, and ask the computer to find groups for us. This is called **clustering**, and K-means is the most common starting point."
      ],
      "id": "15b35285"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans\n",
        "\n",
        "kmeans = KMeans(n_clusters=4, n_init=10)\n",
        "y_pred = kmeans.fit_predict(z)\n",
        "\n",
        "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(13,5.5))\n",
        "axes[0].scatter(z[:,0],z[:,1],c=y_pred)\n",
        "axes[0].set_title(\"K-means' guess (k=4)\",size=15)\n",
        "axes[0].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "\n",
        "axes[1].scatter(z[:,0],z[:,1],c=y)\n",
        "axes[1].set_title(\"The actual truth\",size=15)\n",
        "axes[1].axis(\"equal\")\n",
        "plt.tight_layout()\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "e8d0eb45",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Plain-language idea:** K-means works like this — plant `k` random \"flags\" in the data, assign every point to its nearest flag, move each flag to the average position of the points assigned to it, and repeat until nothing changes. `n_init=10` means we repeat this whole process 10 times from different random starting flags and keep the best result, since K-means can occasionally get \"stuck\" in a mediocre solution depending on where the flags started.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Interpreting this plot:** compare the left panel (K-means' guess, having never seen the true labels) against the right panel (the actual truth). They should look extremely similar in *shape*, even if the color numbers don't match up exactly — K-means has no way of knowing that \"group 0\" in its guess should be called \"group 0\" in the answer key; it can only detect that groups exist, not what to call them. If the shapes visually match, K-means successfully rediscovered the true structure blind.\n",
        "\n",
        "### How many clusters are really there? Two diagnostic tools\n",
        "\n",
        "In real life, we don't get to peek at the answer key. So how would we know to pick `k=4` if we hadn't built this data ourselves? Two common diagnostics:\n",
        "\n",
        "**Diagnostic 1 — The elbow method.** Fit K-means for several different values of `k`, and for each one, measure the total \"wobbliness\" left inside the clusters (technically called *inertia*, or within-cluster sum of squares — basically, how far, on average, points are from their own cluster's center)."
      ],
      "id": "232cdef6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "all_kmeans = [KMeans(n_clusters=i+1, n_init=100).fit(X) for i in range(8)]\n",
        "inertias = [all_kmeans[i].inertia_ for i in range(8)]\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(8,5))\n",
        "plt.plot(np.arange(1,9),inertias,marker='o')\n",
        "plt.title(\"K-means Elbow Plot\",size=16)\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"Number of Clusters (k)\",size=12)\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"Within-Cluster Sum of Squares (lower = tighter clusters)\",size=12)\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "fc9c5de3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this plot:** the line always goes down as `k` increases — that's expected and not informative by itself (more clusters can only ever make each individual cluster tighter, at the extreme limit of \"1 cluster per point\" the wobbliness hits zero). What we're actually looking for is the **elbow** — the point where the line stops dropping steeply and flattens out. That's the signal that adding more clusters beyond that point isn't buying you much. If this plot bends sharply around `k=4`, that's the diagnostic correctly pointing us back toward the true answer, without ever having seen it.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Diagnostic 2 — Silhouette score.** For every point, this measures: \"am I much closer to my own cluster than to the next-nearest cluster?\" It's averaged into one number per value of `k`, ranging from -1 (bad clustering) to +1 (great clustering)."
      ],
      "id": "6e275ab0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score\n",
