      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this plot:** we're looking for the **peak** — the value of `k` with the highest score, meaning clusters are, on average, as tight and well-separated from each other as possible at that setting. A friendly warning worth remembering: silhouette scores can look surprisingly low, even negative, on high-dimensional data *even when the clusters look perfectly clean in a PCA or t-SNE plot*. High dimensionality does strange, counterintuitive things to plain distance measurements. So treat a discouraging silhouette score as one clue among several, not as an automatic verdict.\n",
        "\n",
        "### A more detailed silhouette picture, with the Yellowbrick visualizer\n",
        "\n",
        "The single average silhouette score above hides some detail. **Yellowbrick's `SilhouetteVisualizer`** shows the silhouette score for *every individual point*, grouped by cluster, as a set of horizontal bars — this reveals whether some clusters are much \"worse\" (more mixed-up) than others, which the single average number can hide."
