    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Why we split the data this way:** we shuffle the rows first (so we're not accidentally training only on early-collected wines and testing on later ones), then set aside 80% of wines for *training* the model and hold back the remaining 20% as a *test set* the model never gets to see during training. This mimics the real challenge: correctly classifying a brand-new wine sample using rules learned only from past examples.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Baseline: logistic regression with no regularization\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Modern scikit-learn note\n",
        "Some older tutorials (including the notebook this post is based on) explicitly choose between \"one-vs-rest\" and \"multinomial\" strategies using a `multi_class` argument. Current versions of scikit-learn removed that argument entirely — the library now automatically picks the appropriate multi-class strategy for you. Similarly, `penalty=\"none\"` (a text string) has been replaced by simply passing the Python value `None`.\n",
        ":::"
      ],
      "id": "0cd5be96"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "log_reg = LogisticRegression(penalty=None).fit(X_train,y_train)\n",
        "print(\"Training accuracy:\", round(log_reg.score(X_train,y_train), 3))\n",
        "print(\"Test accuracy:\", round(log_reg.score(X_test,y_test), 3))"
      ],
      "id": "5aac3090",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Interpreting this output:** \"accuracy\" just means the fraction of wines the model correctly classified — 0.95 would mean 95% correct. Watch the relationship between the two numbers: if training accuracy is noticeably higher than test accuracy, that's a warning sign called **overfitting** — the model has essentially \"memorized\" quirks specific to the training wines rather than learning rules that generalize to new ones. With no regularization at all (nothing discouraging the model from fitting every little quirk), some overfitting is common, though this particular dataset is small and clean enough that the gap is often modest.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Adding regularization\n",
        "\n",
        "**Plain-language idea:** regularization is a deliberate \"leash\" on the model — it penalizes the model for relying too heavily on any one measurement, which usually makes it generalize better to new data instead of over-memorizing the training set."
      ],
      "id": "19906303"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "log_reg = LogisticRegression(penalty=\"l1\",C=0.1,solver=\"saga\",max_iter=5000).fit(X_train,y_train)\n",
        "print(\"Training accuracy with L1 regularization (C=0.1):\", round(log_reg.score(X_train,y_train), 3))"
      ],
      "id": "ee0dd2b8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "`C` controls how tight that leash is — and confusingly, **smaller `C` means a *tighter* leash (more regularization)**, not looser. `penalty=\"l1\"` is a specific style of regularization that has a useful side effect: it tends to push many coefficients all the way down to exactly zero, effectively deciding on its own that certain chemical measurements aren't worth using at all. We'll take advantage of that in a moment.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Solver note\n",
        "The original notebook used `solver=\"liblinear\"`, which worked together with the now-removed `multi_class=\"ovr\"` setting. Without that setting, `liblinear` can no longer handle more than 2 classes directly. `solver=\"saga\"` is the modern replacement — it supports the same L1 regularization while handling multi-class problems (like our 3 grape varieties) natively.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "### Letting cross-validation choose the regularization strength for us\n",
        "\n",
        "Rather than guessing a single value of `C`, we can have the computer try several candidate values and pick whichever works best."
